// 测评文章 #6：预算手机
// 数据来源（2026-11-20 核实）：Google Store / Samsung / Motorola 美国规格页、Wirecutter 2026 廉价安卓手机指南、WIRED 2026-10 廉价手机榜、Notebookcheck Galaxy A37 实验室评测、Tom's Guide / TechRadar / Android Authority 单机评测、Amazon 买家评论
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Best Cheap Phones That Survived the 2026 Price Hikes — DealWillow"
  description="The best cheap phones in the US after a year of $100 price hikes: the Pixel 10a still wins on camera and support even at $599, the Galaxy A37 5G is the one that held its price at $449.99, and the Moto G Power costs $299.99."
>
  <main class="wrap article">
    <a class="crumb" href="/">&larr; All reviews</a>

    <div class="head">
      <span class="cat-tag">Phones</span>
      <h1>Best Cheap Phones That Survived the 2026 Price Hikes</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated Nov 2026 · Prices checked Nov 20, 2026 · US models only</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lede">
      <p>
        <strong>The Google Pixel 10a is still the best cheap phone sold in the US — it just isn't a
        cheap phone anymore.</strong> On October 6, 2026, Google raised the 128&nbsp;GB model from $499
        to $599 and the 256&nbsp;GB from $599 to $699, with the hardware unchanged, as memory chip
        prices climbed. If $600 was never your budget, the <strong>Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</strong> is
        the phone that held its ground at $449.99 and comes with six years of Android upgrades. And if
        you want to stay under $300, the <strong>Moto G Power (2026)</strong> at $299.99 still buys two
        days of battery, a headphone jack and a microSD slot — in exchange for a security-update window
        that ends in January 2029.
      </p>
    </div>


    <h2 class="block-title">Everything we recommend</h2>
    <div class="picks">
      <a class="pick" href="#pixel-10a">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best overall</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Best camera and the longest support at this price</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Google Pixel 10a</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$599 · 128 GB (was $499)</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#galaxy-a37">
        <span class="pick-tag alt">Best value</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Held its price, six years of upgrades</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$449.99 · 6 GB / 128 GB</span>
      </a>
      <a class="pick" href="#moto-g-power">
        <span class="pick-tag">Best under $300</span>
        <span class="pick-role">Two-day battery, jack, microSD, IP69</span>
        <span class="pick-name">Moto G Power (2026)</span>
        <span class="pick-price">$299.99 · 8 GB / 128 GB</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ 1. Pixel 10a ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="pixel-10a">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best overall</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">1. Google Pixel 10a</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Price (128 GB)</span><b>$599 — up from $499 on Oct 6, 2026</b></div>
        <div><span>Display</span><b>6.3 in pOLED · 1080 × 2424 · 60–120 Hz</b></div>
        <div><span>Chip / RAM</span><b>Google Tensor G4 · 8 GB</b></div>
        <div><span>Cameras</span><b>48 MP OIS + 13 MP ultrawide · 13 MP front</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery / charging</span><b>5,100 mAh · 30 W wired · 10 W Qi</b></div>
        <div><span>Software support</span><b>7 years of OS and security (to 2033)</b></div>
        <div><span>Durability / weight</span><b>IP68 · Gorilla Glass 7i · 183 g</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery test (press)</span><b>15 h 16 (Tom's Guide, TechRadar)</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Best camera in the price class — the same 48 MP sensor and processing pipeline as the Pixel 9a</li>
            <li>Seven years of OS and security updates, the longest support of anything near this price</li>
            <li>A 60–120 Hz OLED panel, which the 9a never had, plus a brighter 3,000-nit peak mode</li>
            <li>Reviews from Tom's Guide and TechRadar both logged 15 h 16 of battery in their tests</li>
            <li>Clean Android 16 with no duplicate apps and fast day-one updates</li>
            <li>Satellite SOS, IP68 and Gorilla Glass 7i at a mid-range price</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>$100 more than it cost in September, for identical hardware</li>
            <li>Charging is slow: 30 W wired against 45 W on the Samsung below</li>
            <li>The in-display fingerprint reader is the single most common owner complaint</li>
            <li>No telephoto lens, and 8× is digital crop from the main sensor</li>
            <li>128 GB with no microSD slot, on a phone meant to last seven years</li>
            <li>Runs warm under sustained load, and Tensor G4 is a generation behind</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          The case for the 10a was never the chip — it is the Tensor G4 that shipped in the Pixel 9,
          and Tom's Guide called this "the laziest upgrade" of the season. The case is everything
          wrapped around it: a 6.3-inch OLED that finally runs at 120 Hz, a 5,100 mAh battery that both
          Tom's Guide and TechRadar measured at 15 hours 16 minutes, a 48 MP main camera with optical
          stabilisation, and seven years of updates that run to 2033. That last number is the one that
          matters in a year when the price of a cheap phone went up.
        </p>
        <p>
          What changed in October is the arithmetic. At $499 this was an easy recommendation; at $599
          it is competing with phones that were mid-range a year ago, and the $100 gap to the Galaxy
          A37 buys a bigger battery, faster charging and a plastic frame instead of a Pixel camera.
          Google has not said publicly why it raised the price on a seven-month-old phone, but the
          timing lines up with the memory shortage that pushed DRAM costs from roughly $2.80 to $12
          per gigabyte, and Samsung and Apple raised prices on older models the same month. Treat the
          $599 as a sticker price, not a floor: this phone has already been discounted once this year,
          and Black Friday is a week away.
        </p>
        <p>
          The recurring complaint in owner reviews is the fingerprint reader — the same optical
          in-display sensor that reviewers flagged on the 9a. Amazon reviews from verified purchases
          describe it failing repeatedly after re-registering prints, and Reddit threads from February
          through September 2026 repeat the theme. Charging is the other consistent gripe: 30 W wired
          is fine overnight and frustrating at an airport. Both are software-and-habit problems rather
          than defects, but if you unlock your phone fifty times a day, add face unlock on day one.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the best camera, the longest support and the cleanest software under $600 — buy it on sale, and buy the 256 GB version if you plan to keep it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 2. Galaxy A37 ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="galaxy-a37">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best value</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">2. Samsung Galaxy A37 5G</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Price (6 GB / 128 GB)</span><b>$449.99 · $540 for 8 GB / 256 GB</b></div>
        <div><span>Display</span><b>6.7 in Super AMOLED · 1080p · 120 Hz</b></div>
        <div><span>Chip / RAM</span><b>Exynos 1480 · 6 GB (8 GB on 256 GB)</b></div>
        <div><span>Cameras</span><b>50 MP OIS + 8 MP ultrawide + 5 MP macro · 12 MP front</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery / charging</span><b>5,000 mAh · 45 W wired · no wireless</b></div>
        <div><span>Software support</span><b>6 major Android upgrades</b></div>
        <div><span>Durability / weight</span><b>IP68 · Gorilla Glass Victus+ both sides · 196 g</b></div>
        <div><span>Missing</span><b>No microSD, no headphone jack, no charger in box</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>$449.99 today, the same as its April launch price — it did not get hiked</li>
            <li>Six major Android upgrades from Android 16, so it should stay current into the early 2030s</li>
            <li>IP68 water resistance and Gorilla Glass Victus+ on both faces, rare at this price</li>
            <li>45 W charging, and Notebookcheck measured a full charge in under 70 minutes</li>
            <li>Bright 120 Hz AMOLED with stereo speakers — good for video, not just text</li>
            <li>Notebookcheck found excellent call quality and dual eSIM support</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>The Exynos 1480 is a full performance tier below the Pixel 10a, per Android Authority</li>
            <li>Fingerprint sensor is slow and inconsistent — a repeated complaint in owner forums</li>
            <li>Base model pairs 6 GB of RAM with a heavy One UI skin, and 128 GB leaves about 114 GB usable</li>
            <li>Gets noticeably warm and sluggish after 15–20 minutes of hard use</li>
            <li>No wireless charging, no microSD, no headphone jack, and no charger in the box</li>
            <li>At full price Android Authority would rather you waited for a discount below $400</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          This is the phone that makes the October price hikes look like a choice rather than a law.
          The A37 launched in April at $449.99 and it is still $449.99 in November, which in 2026
          counts as a discount. For that money you get a 6.7-inch 120 Hz AMOLED, IP68, Victus+ glass
          front and back, 45 W charging, and six major Android upgrades — the longest support outside
          the Pixel, and three times what the Moto below offers.
        </p>
        <p>
          Be clear-eyed about the chip. Android Authority's three-week review puts the Exynos 1480 a
          full tier below the Pixel 10a: messaging, maps and video are fine, but the keyboard
          occasionally lags, switching between cameras hitches, and a 20-minute Android Auto session
          left the phone hot and sluggish while the navigation itself kept running. Notebookcheck's
          benchmarks put the same chip ahead of the A36's Snapdragon and behind on demanding games.
          Neither reviewer calls it unusable. Both call it the ceiling.
        </p>
        <p>
          The fingerprint reader is the recurring owner complaint, and it is not a one-off: a Samsung
          community thread from August 2026 documents failed enrolments and missed unlocks on a
          full-price unit, Android Authority switched to pattern unlock, and user reviews on
          aggregator sites say the same thing in different words. The other honest caveat is storage:
          the $449.99 model is 6 GB of RAM and 128 GB with no microSD slot, and the 256 GB version
          costs $540 — which is most of the way back to a Pixel.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the best phone here for the money, as long as you accept a mid-tier chip and buy the 256 GB model if you keep phones for five years.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 3. Moto G Power ============ -->
    <section class="product" id="moto-g-power">
      <h2 class="prod-role">Best under $300</h2>
      <h3 class="prod-name">3. Moto G Power (2026)</h3>

      <div class="specs">
        <div><span>Price (8 GB / 128 GB)</span><b>$299.99 · as low as $249 on sale</b></div>
        <div><span>Display</span><b>6.8 in IPS LCD · 1080p · 120 Hz · 1,000 nits</b></div>
        <div><span>Chip / RAM</span><b>MediaTek Dimensity 6300 · 8 GB</b></div>
        <div><span>Cameras</span><b>50 MP OIS + 8 MP ultrawide · 32 MP front · 1080p video</b></div>
        <div><span>Battery / charging</span><b>5,200 mAh · 30 W wired · no wireless</b></div>
        <div><span>Software support</span><b>1 OS upgrade · security to Jan 2029</b></div>
        <div><span>Durability / weight</span><b>IP68 + IP69 · MIL-STD-810H · 208 g</b></div>
        <div><span>Extras</span><b>microSD to 1 TB · 3.5 mm jack · stereo speakers</b></div>
      </div>

      <div class="proscons">
        <div class="pros">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to buy</p>
          <ul>
            <li>$299.99 in a year when most rivals added $100 — the only pick here that is still cheap</li>
            <li>5,200 mAh rated for up to two days, with 30 W wired charging</li>
            <li>IP68 and IP69 plus MIL-STD-810H testing claims and Gorilla Glass 7i</li>
            <li>Keeps the microSD slot and the 3.5 mm headphone jack that both phones above dropped</li>
            <li>50 MP main camera with optical stabilisation, which the 2025 model lacked</li>
            <li>Vegan-leather back that resists fingerprints and grips without a case</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cons">
          <p class="pc-title">Reasons to avoid</p>
          <ul>
            <li>One OS upgrade and security patches only through January 2029 — the shortest support here</li>
            <li>Dimmer LCD with weaker contrast than either AMOLED phone above</li>
            <li>Dimensity 6300 is mid-tier silicon, and gaming stutters</li>
            <li>Video tops out at 1080p; there is no 4K recording and no zoom lens</li>
            <li>208 g is heavy for its size, and it is a two-handed phone</li>
            <li>Wireless charging was removed this year; no charger in the box</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="body">
        <p>
          Motorola did the unfashionable thing and held the price: $299.99 at launch in January 2026,
          $299.99 in November, with tracked discounts down to roughly $249. For that you get a
          6.8-inch 120 Hz screen, 5,200 mAh, a 50 MP stabilised camera, IP68 plus IP69, and the two
          ports everyone else deleted — microSD and a headphone jack. As a phone for a teenager, a
          work truck or a parent who wants to stop thinking about charging, it is the most practical
          thing on this page.
        </p>
        <p>
          The catch is how long it stays practical. Motorola's support tracker lists one OS upgrade,
          to Android 17, and security patches through January 2029 — three years, against six on the
          Samsung and seven on the Pixel. Wirecutter reached the same conclusion about the cheaper
          Moto G and G Play, whose updates stop in late 2028; that short horizon makes a $300 phone
          more expensive per year of safe use than a $450 one. If you replace phones every two years,
          none of this matters. If you keep them, it is the whole decision.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two details to check at the register. First, wireless charging: the 2025 model had 15 W
          wireless charging and Motorola removed it for 2026, yet at least one retail write-up still
          lists it as included, so confirm against the model number rather than the spec sheet you
          land on. Second, the display is LCD, not OLED — 1,000 nits is bright enough outdoors but the
          blacks are grey, and if you watch a lot of video on your phone the two AMOLED picks above are
          worth the extra money.
        </p>
        <p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> the last genuinely cheap phone standing — buy it if the price is the point and you replace phones every couple of years.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Also worth knowing</h2>
      <p>
        Two more names sit just outside the picks. The <strong>Samsung Galaxy A27 5G</strong> is
        announced at $350 with a Snapdragon 6 Gen 3, which should be a real step up from the A26's
        Exynos — but Wirecutter notes it also arrives with lower-resolution cameras, weaker
        waterproofing, one colour, a thicker body and a $50 higher price, and it had not been tested
        when this was written, so treat it as a lead. Below that, the <strong>Moto G (2026)</strong>
        at $300 and the <strong>Moto G Play (2026)</strong> at $250 performed almost identically in
        Wirecutter's testing and both stutter under load, with security updates ending in late 2028;
        the G Power above is the better $300.
      </p>
      <p>
        Context helps here. The price increases are not a Samsung or Google decision so much as a
        memory-market one: the same DRAM shortage pushed Apple and Samsung to raise prices on models
        already on sale, and Wirecutter describes new budget phones arriving with worse cameras and
        worse water resistance for more money. That is why this guide ranks phones by how long they
        stay supported as much as by what they cost today.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">How we picked</h2>
      <p>
        Sources: Wirecutter's budget Android guide and WIRED's cheap-phone guide for the shortlist;
        Notebookcheck's lab review of the Galaxy A37, Tom's Guide and TechRadar's Pixel 10a tests and
        Android Authority's three-week A37 review for measured results; then every spec re-checked
        against Google, Samsung and Motorola's own US product pages.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prices were checked on <strong>Nov 20, 2026</strong>. One note on sourcing: WIRED's Pixel 10a
        price widget reads $595–$599 while its own text still says $499 — that is the October hike
        showing up in one place and not the other, not a disagreement about the phone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sec">
      <h2 class="block-title">Before you buy: two things almost nobody tells you</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>128 GB is a five-year trap.</strong> Neither the Pixel nor the Galaxy takes a microSD
        card, and on the A37 about 114 GB of the 128 is usable once Android and One UI are installed.
        If you plan to keep the phone past 2029, the 256 GB version is not an upsell — it is the
        difference between keeping your photos and paying for cloud storage you did not want. The
        Moto is the exception: it takes a card up to 1 TB for $20.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The sticker price moved twice this year, so check the date on the price.</strong>
        Google's $100 increase took effect October 6 and applies to stock already on shelves; Samsung
        and Apple raised prices on older models the same month. Retail listings, review articles and
        even a publication's own price widget can lag the change by weeks. Also budget for a charger:
        none of these three ships with one, and the 45 W brick Samsung wants for the A37's fast
        charging is sold separately — the USB-C power banks we track on our
        <a href="/amazon/">Amazon deals page</a> are a cheaper way to keep one in your bag.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

